# 000800-000899/20181005T083655Z_862579095.wiki
zΔ⚡b|+{{About%7Cthe file comparison utility%7Cdata comparisons in general%7Cdata comparison%7Cdiffs in Wikipedia%7C:help:diff%7Cother uses%7CDIFF (disambiguation){{!}}DIFF}}%0A{{lowercase%7Ctitle%3Ddiff}}%0A%0A{{Infobox software%0A%7C name                   %3D diff%0A%7C title                  %3D diff%0A%7C screenshot             %3D <!%2D%2D Image name is enough %2D%2D>%0A%7C caption                %3D %0A%7C screenshot size        %3D %0A%7C screenshot alt         %3D %0A%7C collapsible            %3D %0A%7C author                 %3D [[Douglas McIlroy]]%0A%7C developer              %3D %0A%7C released               %3D {{Start date and age%7C1974}}%0A%7C latest release version %3D %0A%7C latest release date    %3D <!%2D%2D {{Start date and age%7CYYYY%7CMM%7CDD%7Cdf%3Dyes/no}} %2D%2D>%0A%7C status                 %3D %0A%7C programming language   %3D %0A%7C platform               %3D [[Unix]], [[Unix%2Dlike]] systems%0A%7C genre                  %3D [[Command%2Dline utility]]%0A%7C license                %3D %0A%7C website                %3D %0A%7C standard               %3D %0A%7C AsOf                   %3D %0A}}%0A%0AIn [[computing]], the '''<tt>diff</tt> utility''' is a [[data comparison]] tool that calculates and displays the differences between two files. Unlike [[edit distance]] notions used for other purposes, <tt>diff</tt> is line%2Doriented rather than character%2Doriented, but it is like [[Levenshtein distance]] in that it tries to determine the smallest set of deletions and insertions to create one file from the other.%0AThe <tt>diff</tt> command displays the changes made in a standard format, such that both humans and machines can understand the changes and apply them: given one file and the changes, the other file can be created.%0A%0ATypically, <tt>diff</tt> is used to show the changes between two versions of the same file. Modern implementations also support [[binary file]]s.<ref>MacKenzie ''et al.''  "Binary Files and Forcing Text Comparison" in ''Comparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch''.  Downloaded 28 April 2007.  [https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Binary.html]</ref> The output is called a "diff", or a [[patch (computing)%7Cpatch]], since the output can be applied with the [[Unix]] program <tt>[[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]]</tt>.%0AThe output of similar file comparison utilities are also called a "diff"; like the use of the word "[[grep]]" for describing the act of searching, the word ''diff'' became a generic term for calculating data difference and the results thereof.<ref>Eric S. Raymond (ed.), [http://catb.org/jargon/html/D/diff.html &ldquo;diff&rdquo;], ''The Jargon File'', version 4.4.7</ref>%0A%0A%3D%3D History %3D%3D%0AThe <tt>diff</tt> utility was developed in the early 1970s on the Unix operating system which was emerging from [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey. The final version, first shipped with the 5th Edition of Unix in 1974, was entirely written by [[Douglas McIlroy]].%0AThis research was published in a 1976 paper co%2Dwritten with James W. Hunt who developed an initial prototype of <tt>diff</tt>.<ref name%3D"diff paper">{{cite journal%7Cauthor1%3DJames W. Hunt %7Cauthor2%3DM. Douglas McIlroy %7Ctitle%3DAn Algorithm for Differential File Comparison%7Cvolume%3D41%7Cjournal%3DComputing Science Technical Report, Bell Laboratories%7Cdate%3DJune 1976%7Cpages%3D%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.cs.dartmouth.edu/~doug/diff.pdf}}</ref>%0AThe algorithm this paper described became known as the [[Hunt–McIlroy algorithm]].%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen C. Johnson%7CSteve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s <tt>proof</tt> program.%0A<tt>Proof</tt> also originated on Unix and, like <tt>diff</tt>, produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "&lt;") for presenting line insertions and deletions in the program's output.%0AThe [[heuristic]]s used in these early applications were, however, deemed unreliable.%0AThe potential usefulness of a diff tool provoked McIlroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well in the processing and size limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware.%0AHis approach to the problem resulted from collaboration also with individuals at Bell Labs including [[Alfred Aho]], Elliot Pinson, [[Jeffrey Ullman]], and Harold S. Stone.%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of the <tt>[[ed (Unix)%7Ced]]</tt> line editor provided <tt>diff</tt> with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".%0AThese edit scripts, when saved to a file, can, along with the original file, be reconstituted by <tt>ed</tt> into the modified file in its entirety.%0AThis greatly reduced the [[secondary storage]] necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.%0AMcIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for <tt>diff</tt> where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have <tt>diff</tt> be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the <tt>ed</tt> command.%0A%0ALate in 1984 [[Larry Wall]] created a separate utility, [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]], releasing its source code on the ''mod.sources'' and ''net.sources'' newsgroups.<ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D A patch applier%2D%2DYOU WANT THIS!!!%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D November 9, 1984%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 1457@sdcrdcf.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/net.sources/qtfVio1sSHs/G0cPT5HFDFcJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref><ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D patch version 1.2%2D%2DYOU WANT THIS%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D November 29, 1984%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 1508@sdcrdcf.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/net.sources/uWFr9NOp_fw/SRS_P2vSgFgJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref><ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D patch version 1.3%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D May 8, 1985%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 813@genrad.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/mod.sources/xSQM63e39YY/apNNJSkJi0gJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref>%0AThis program generalized and extended the ability to modify files with output from <tt>diff</tt>.%0A%0AModes in [[Emacs]] also allow for converting the format of patches and even editing patches interactively.%0A%0AIn <tt>diff</tt>'s early years, common uses included comparing changes in the source of software code and markup for technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.%0AThe output targeted for <tt>ed</tt> was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.%0AThe [[Source Code Control System]] (SCCS) and its ability to archive revisions emerged in the late 1970s as a consequence of storing edit scripts from <tt>diff</tt>.%0A%0A%3D%3D Algorithm %3D%3D%0AThe operation of <tt>diff</tt> is based on solving the [[longest common subsequence problem]].<ref name%3D"diff paper" />%0A%0AIn this problem, given two sequences of items:%0A%0A        {{underline%7Ca}} {{underline%7Cb}} {{underline%7Cc}} {{underline%7Cd}} {{underline%7Cf}} {{underline%7Cg}} h {{underline%7Cj}} q {{underline%7Cz}}%0A%0A        {{underline%7Ca}} {{underline%7Cb}} {{underline%7Cc}} {{underline%7Cd}} e {{underline%7Cf}} {{underline%7Cg}} i {{underline%7Cj}} k r x y {{underline%7Cz}}%0A%0Aand we want to find a longest sequence of items that is present in both original sequences in the same order. That is, we want to find a new sequence which can be obtained from the first original sequence by deleting some items, and from the second original sequence by deleting other items. We also want this sequence to be as long as possible. In this case it is%0A%0A        a b c d  f  g  j  z%0A%0AFrom a longest common subsequence it is only a small step to get <tt>diff</tt>%2Dlike output: if an item is absent in the subsequence but present in the first original sequence, it must have been deleted (as indicated by the '%2D' marks, below). If it is absent in the subsequence but present in the second original sequence, it must have been inserted (as indicated by the '%2B' marks).%0A%0A        e   h i   q   k r x y%0A        %2B   %2D %2B   %2D   %2B %2B %2B %2B%0A%0A%3D%3D Usage %3D%3D%0AThe <tt>diff</tt> command is invoked from the command line, passing it the names of two files: <tt>diff ''original'' ''new''</tt>. The output of the command represents the changes required to transform the ''original'' file into the ''new'' file.%0A%0AIf ''original'' and ''new'' are directories, then <tt>diff</tt> will be run on each file that exists in both directories. An option, <tt>%2Dr</tt>, will recursively descend any matching subdirectories to compare files between directories.%0A%0AAny of the examples in the article use the following two files, ''original'' and ''new'':%0A{{Col%2Dbegin}}%0A{{Col%2Dbreak%7Cwidth%3D33%25}}%0A''original'':%0A<source lang%3D"text" line>%0AThis part of the%0Adocument has stayed the%0Asame from version to%0Aversion.  It shouldn't%0Abe shown if it doesn't%0Achange.  Otherwise, that%0Awould not be helping to%0Acompress the size of the%0Achanges.%0A%0AThis paragraph contains%0Atext that is outdated.%0AIt will be deleted in the%0Anear future.%0A%0AIt is important to spell%0Acheck this dokument. On%0Athe other hand, a%0Amisspelled word isn't%0Athe end of the world.%0ANothing in the rest of%0Athis paragraph needs to%0Abe changed. Things can%0Abe added after it.%0A</source>%0A{{col%2Dbreak}}%0A''new'':%0A<source lang%3D"text" line>%0AThis is an important%0Anotice! It should%0Atherefore be located at%0Athe beginning of this%0Adocument!%0A%0AThis part of the%0Adocument has stayed the%0Asame from version to%0Aversion.  It shouldn't%0Abe shown if it doesn't%0Achange.  Otherwise, that%0Awould not be helping to%0Acompress the size of the%0Achanges.%0A%0AIt is important to spell%0Acheck this document. On%0Athe other hand, a%0Amisspelled word isn't%0Athe end of the world.%0ANothing in the rest of%0Athis paragraph needs to%0Abe changed. Things can%0Abe added after it.%0A%0AThis paragraph contains%0Aimportant new additions%0Ato this document.%0A</source>%0A{{col%2Dbreak%7Cwidth%3D33%25}}%0AThe command '''<tt>diff original new</tt>''' produces the following ''normal diff output'':%0A{{pre%7C%0A0a1,6%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C> This is an important%0A> notice! It should%0A> therefore be located at%0A> the beginning of this%0A> document!%0A>}}%0A11,15d16%0A{{font color%7Cdarkred%7C< This paragraph contains%0A< text that is outdated.%0A< It will be deleted in the%0A< near future.%0A<}}%0A17c18%0A{{font color%7Cdarkred%7C< check this dokument. On}}%0A%2D%2D%2D%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C> check this document. On}}%0A24a26,29%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C>%0A> This paragraph contains%0A> important new additions%0A> to this document.}}}}%0A%0A{{Note2}} ''Here, the diff output is shown with colors to make it easier to read. The diff utility does not produce colored output; its output is [[plain text]]. However, many tools can show the output with colors by using [[syntax highlighting]].''%0A{{col%2Dend}}%0AIn this traditional output format, '''<tt>a</tt>''' stands for ''added'', '''<tt>d</tt>''' for ''deleted'' and '''<tt>c</tt>''' for ''changed''. Line numbers of the original file appear before <tt>a</tt>/<tt>d</tt>/<tt>c</tt> and those of the new file appear after. The [[Less%2Dthan sign%7Cless%2Dthan]] and [[Greater%2Dthan sign%7Cgreater%2Dthan]] signs (at the beginning of lines that are added, deleted or changed) indicate which file the li|=47c8|
